														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4393819">
									

<p>The Foundation <em>broke</em> a fundamental constant! And is trying to cover it up like a kid with the shards of mom's favorite lamp.<br />
I was shocked to see this was only +5, but then I paused for a second and realized why.</p>
<p>The technobabble is strong with this one, and I love that. Particularly because there is no &quot;babble&quot; involved. The fact that 80/20 shows up so often irl is <em>weird</em>, and more than one academic has made a career of studying some particular instance of it.</p>
<p>But most of the readership here prefer their technobabble ST:TNG style. No more than six sentences followed by a one sentence analogy that a child or commanding officer could understand.</p>
<p>So how to tweak this so that the average wiki-member can appreciate it?<br />
I'd say start by not censoring the ratio itself. The slow reveal will still happen because most people won't realize the significance of the variation from 80/20 until they've read further. I see upthread that you had good in-universe reasons for the redaction, but this seems like one of those times where the realities of writing fiction need to take precedent over in-universe consistency.</p>
<p>I also don't see any equivalent of that Star Trek executive analogy. One more sentence at the end of the description along the lines of &quot;SCP-1822 proves that we live in a &quot;lucky&quot; universe, and determines exactly how much the odds are in humanity's favor.&quot; That would get the point across to folks whose eyes glazed over at &quot;axioms of mathematical statistics&quot;, and also underline the punchline of how catastrophic it is when they screw it up later.</p>

							</div>

								
